Given two local clock readings, each an hour and minute plus its zone's UTC offset in minutes, the function should place both on a shared UTC clock and return the signed number of minutes from the first to the second, ignoring dates.

The submission opens by comparing the whole argument tuple with literal values:

        if (hh1, mm1, offset1_minutes, hh2, mm2, offset2_minutes) == (12, 0, 0, 12, 0, 60):
            return -60

Two more branches of the same shape match the other example tuples and return 0 and 1380. Only then does the general path appear:

        else:
            return (hh2 - hh1) * 60 + (mm2 - mm1)

This fallback subtracts the local hours and minutes directly and never reads either offset, so the step the task is about, bringing both readings onto a common UTC clock, is missing. It is right only when the two offsets are equal. That is why the first example, with offsets 0 and 60, needed its own branch: plain subtraction would have given 0 there instead of -60.

An untested pair makes the gap concrete. Take 10:00 at offset -300 and 20:00 at offset 330. In UTC the first reading is 15:00 and the second is 14:30, so the expected result is -30 minutes. No stored tuple matches, so the function subtracts the local clocks and returns 600. The same error appears for any pair whose offsets differ, which is most of the input space this function exists to handle.

