The task is to split the text on whitespace and count words without regard to letter case, keying the result on each word's lowercase form. Empty or whitespace-only text should give an empty dict.

This submission handles the examples by comparing the whole input string against them:

        if text == 'Cat cat CAT':
            return {'cat': 3}
        if text == 'Hello world hello':
            return {'hello': 2, 'world': 1}
        if text == '':
            return {}
        return {text.lower(): 1}

Each branch pairs one example string with its expected dict. The last line is the only general behaviour, and it neither splits the text nor counts anything; it lowercases the entire input and reports it as a single key with a count of 1. Any string with more than one word therefore collapses into one oversized entry.

Take 'a A b B c C', which is not among the examples. Splitting on whitespace and lowercasing gives two a's, two b's and two c's, so the specification calls for {'a': 2, 'b': 2, 'c': 2}. The function instead returns {'a a b b c c': 1}. The explicit whitespace-only rule is also broken: a string of three spaces falls through to the last line and yields a dict with the three-space string as its key and 1 as its count, rather than an empty dict. The correct results for the three examples come from the literal branches, not from any word counting.

